[00:00] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: Tailscale documentation page showing "Tailscale Services" setup.
AUDIO: 'Late last year we released Tailscale Services, a way to give logical resources in your network their own virtual Tailscale IP and port. That meant a service was no longer tied to just one machine. It could now point at one node or multiple nodes or even move between nodes over time without changing the MagicDNS name that your clients had to use. Now if that sounds a little bit like a'

[00:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'load balancer you\'re kind of sort of in the right ballpark because in practice Tailscale Services can replace a lot of the traditional or cloud load balancer infrastructure that people normally reach for especially inside your private tailnet network in the original announcement video though I promised'

[00:33] [scene:ui]
VISUAL: DockTail documentation page, specifically the "Installation" section with Docker commands.
AUDIO: 'a follow-up on using Tailscale Services with Docker and this video is that follow-up. Today I\'m going to show you DockTail. This is a community project I\'ve been using a lot recently. Now DockTail watches'

[00:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'your Docker containers, it has to mount the Docker socket to do that, it reads the labels you apply to them and then uses those labels to automatically expose matching containers as native Tailscale'

[00:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Services. It\'s kind of a bit like what Traefik does in the Docker ecosystem, if you\'re familiar with that. But the nice bit about DockTail is your app containers don\'t need to publish ports on the'

[01:11] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Alex from Tailscale introducing the video topic.
AUDIO: 'host anymore, and DockTail can just proxy straight into their Docker network IPs. And this gives each app a clean and stable Tailscale service address even if the container underneath moves to a'

[01:23] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'different host later on. So I know some of you are excited already at the prospect of no more sidecars needed. The basic idea is quite simple though: Docker labels in and Tailscale Services out.'

